Executive Overview and State of Bio-Hybrid Renal Replacement Therapy
End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) affects over two million individuals worldwide, representing an immense clinical and economic burden. Standard clinic-based hemodialysis remains the primary renal replacement modality; however, its intermittent schedule—typically three sessions per week—subjects the cardiovascular system to severe fluid shifts and toxic uremic surges. This regime results in a five-year mortality rate ranging between 50% and 65%, a clinical outcome worse than many metastasized solid tumors, accompanied by an annual mortality risk of 15% to 20%. Every 48 hours, patients accumulate significant fluid and metabolic waste, followed by violent ultrashort ultrafiltration that induces systemic vascular calcification, cognitive decline, and chronic myocardial stunning. The resulting post-dialysis fatigue ("dialysis hangover") routinely consumes six to twelve hours per session, severely restricting patient autonomy and quality of life.
To address these limitations, biomedical research has transitioned toward continuous, wearable bio-hybrid artificial kidney architectures. Operating continuously at native renal flow velocities, these systems eliminate intermittent fluid spikes and maintain constant physiological homeostasis. By combining Micro-Electromechanical Systems (MEMS) hemofiltration, non-thrombogenic zwitterionic surface chemistry, photoelectrocatalytic (PEC) urea breakdown, and living proximal tubule epithelial cell bioreactors, next-generation bio-hybrid devices offer survival profiles comparable to living-donor kidney transplants.
