MEMS Silicon Nanopore Membranes and Zwitterionic Surface Coating
The primary hemofiltration unit utilizes a Micro-Electromechanical Systems (MEMS) engineered Silicon Nanopore Membrane (SNM). The membrane features uniform 5\text{ to }7\text{ nm} rectangular slit pores etched into a single-crystal silicon substrate, providing high hydraulic permeability while retaining essential serum proteins such as albumin.
All blood-contacting silicon surfaces are covalently modified with a sub-5 nm zwitterionic poly(sulfobetaine methacrylate) (pSBMA) hydrogel monolayer. The zwitterionic coating forms an electrostatically bound hydration shell that prevents protein adsorption, platelet activation, and thrombus formation, eliminating the need for continuous systemic heparin administration.
Piezo-Acoustic Micro-Vortex Defouling (DF-IPSS)
To prevent concentration polarization and protein cake accumulation, an array of thin-film Aluminum Nitride (AlN) piezoelectric transducers is bonded to the membrane frame. Operating on a Dual-Frequency Interleaved Phase-Shifted Sweep (DF-IPSS) protocol (40\text{ to }100\text{ kHz}), the transducers generate standing Surface Acoustic Waves (SAWs) across the fluid-membrane interface.
Acoustic streaming generates micro-vortices that lift accumulated protein layers off the membrane. The acoustic intensity is capped below a strict hemolysis threshold (E_{ac} < 0.25\text{ W/cm}^2), while Transmembrane Pressure (TMP) is maintained below 18.0\text{ mmHg}. A duty cycle of 15\text{ ms} active pulse followed by an 85\text{ ms} relaxation interval prevents localized thermal dissipation issues.
As a secondary mechanical fallback, a zwitterionic-coated micro-wiper containing embedded superparamagnetic nanoparticles rests inside the fluid chamber. An external linear magnetic actuator translates a localized magnetic field to drag the internal scraper across the membrane, physically shearing stubborn protein deposits without breaching the sterile fluid boundary.
Photoelectrocatalytic (PEC) Urea Sublimation
To eliminate bulky dialysate fluid tanks, urea (CO(NH_2)_2) elimination is performed using a photoelectrocatalytic (PEC) flow cell. The cell utilizes a nanostructured hematite (\alpha\text{-Fe}_2\text{O}_3) photoanode coated with a nickel oxyhydroxide (NiOOH) catalyst. Driven by continuous 460\text{ nm} blue LED illumination, the cell oxidizes urea directly into gaseous nitrogen (N_2) and carbon dioxide (CO_2) without external voltage bias:
The evolved gases vent through hydrophobic polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) membranes, maintaining steady system pressures.
Proximal Tubule Bioreactor (RPTEC/TERT1)
To perform biological reabsorption, the filtrate flows through a bioreactor housing conditionally immortalized human proximal tubule epithelial cells (RPTEC/TERT1). The cells are cultured under continuous physiological fluid shear stress (0.2\text{ to }0.5\text{ dyne/cm}^2), stimulating differentiated 3D tubular lumen formation, tight junction expression, and active reabsorption of glucose, bicarbonate, and essential electrolytes.
Dermal Integration (UGIRAP Gecko Patch)
The Universal Gecko-Inspired Reversible Adhesive Platform (UGIRAP) anchors the wearable device to the patient's epidermis without chemical adhesives. Millions of flexible PDMS micro-setae terminating in nanoscale spatulae exploit van der Waals forces, providing a dry shear strength \ge 5.2\text{ N/cm}^2. Interspersed microfluidic channels (10\text{ to }50\ \mu\text{m} diameter) wick perspiration away passively via capillary action into a peripheral absorbent hydrogel, preventing tissue maceration. Peeling the patch at a 45^\circ angle alters the contact geometry, collapsing the van der Waals interface for pain-free removal without skin tearing.
Deterministic Control Kernel (ce_os) and Safety Firewall
System monitoring is executed by the ce_os deterministic operating system kernel, implemented in bare-metal, no_std Rust running on a 32-bit RISC-V microcontroller. Operating on a hard real-time 10 ms control loop, ce_os monitors inlet pressure, outlet pressure, acoustic power, and optical blood leak detectors. If critical anomalies occur—such as micro-air embolisms or acoustic spikes exceeding 0.25\text{ W/cm}^2—the CE-OS Deterministic Safety Firewall triggers an electromagnetic bypass line-block within 9.15\text{ ns} via Rayleigh cavitation collapse logic, isolating the patient loop.
